From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tariq Bashir <tbashir@brightsign.biz>
Date: Wed, 4 Mar 2026 20:58:35 +0000
Subject: feat: Brightsign optimisations for showing window using opacity
 change
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The main changes of this patch are:

1) In Chromium, EffectTree::UpdateIsDrawn() sets is_drawn=false
for any EffectNode with screen_space_opacity==0. This cascades through
the entire rendering pipeline:

  - LayerShouldBeSkippedForDrawPropertiesComputation() skips the layer
  - contributes_to_drawn_render_surface is set to false
  - HasValidTilePriorities() returns false
  - UpdateTiles() is never called (no raster work scheduled)
  - Tile resources may be released entirely

On BrightSign, windows are set to opacity=0 to hide them while content
loads, then restored to opacity=255 to reveal the page. With the
above mentioned optimization, setting opacity=0 causes all compositing, tiling,
and raster work to stop. When opacity is restored, the compositor must
re-rasterize all tiles from scratch, causing a visible delay before the
page appears.

This change adds an exception for "window-level" effect nodes — those
whose parent is the root (kRootPropertyNodeId, id=0) or secondary root
(kSecondaryRootPropertyNodeId, id=1) of the effect tree. These nodes
correspond to the browser compositor's content window layer, whose
opacity is set via DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::SetOpacity() ->
ui::Layer::SetOpacity() -> cc::Layer::SetOpacity().

CSS element opacity (e.g. a <div> with opacity:0) creates effect nodes
deeper in the renderer compositor's tree (parent_id > 1), so those
still receive the is_drawn=false optimization as before.

The net effect is:
  - Window opacity=0: is_drawn stays true, compositor continues to
    composite and rasterize tiles. The output is still visually
    transparent (screen_space_opacity=0 is still computed). When
    opacity is restored, the page displays on the next frame.
  - CSS element opacity=0: is_drawn=false as usual, tiles are skipped.
    No behavior change from upstream.

2) This fix makes sure that when the opacity is set to 0 that both the
top level surface and subsurfaces end up with opacity 0 in weston,
so we don't end up assigning planes to them.

3) Setting the Aura layer to transparent makes the GPU compositor produce a
transparent buffer. With immediate alpha commits, that stale transparent
buffer can be briefly shown when the window is made visible again.

Keep the Aura layer opacity always set to fully opaque to avoid this.
The window transparency is now completely handled by nexus or weston.

4) Add an explicit CommitOpacity() path in WaylandSurface to flush pending
alpha/blending updates without waiting for the next frame submission.

Use this in WaylandToplevelWindow::SetOpacity() to update both the primary
subsurface (where content is rendered) and the root surface. Commit the
subsurface first and the root last so synchronized subsurface state is
applied in the same repaint cycle.

This avoids stale subsurface opacity until a later GPU frame and improves
show/hide coordination across related surfaces.

In order to ensure that the first frame displayed after calling Show is
not transparent a separate patch has been added to change the Aura compositor
layer opacity to always be 1.0. I.e. we are now completely controlling window
opacity through the Wayland surface. PlatformWindow::SetOpacity will get called
with the Aura window compositor opacity (i.e. with 1.0 and not the requested
opacity) when there is a compositor update and we ignore this by removing the
updates in WaylandSurface::ApplyPendingState. The only way to update the window
opacity is through the WaylandToplevelWindow::SetOpacity call.

